{
  "topic": "proper fractions",
  "affirmation": "You taught fractions with warm visual models and a relatable cake example.",
  "identity": "You teach by making the abstract visible: you draw and partition shapes, ask students to count aloud, and tie the idea to everyday life (like cutting a cake). Your tone is patient and encouraging, and you repeatedly name the math vocabulary so students hear the language of fractions.",
  "moments": [
    {
      "title": "Shape identification",
      "quote": "یہ سرکل ہے۔",
      "why": "That quick, correct shape call showed you open the lesson by lowering the barrier—students could name what they saw and join the math work immediately."
    },
    {
      "title": "Notation recall",
      "quote": "10 میں سے 9، 9، 10 میں سے 1 گئے تو 9 بچ گیا۔ 9 اور 10 نیچے، اور 9 اوپر۔",
      "why": "This muddled but honest attempt revealed a real student idea about numerator/denominator placement that you caught and began to clarify."
    },
    {
      "title": "Cake anchor",
      "quote": "ہم پارٹ کرتے ہیں۔",
      "why": "That everyday reply let you connect partitioning to fairness and sharing, which made the math feel sensible and familiar."
    }
  ],
  "strength_name": "clear visual models",
  "strength_note": "You repeatedly partitioned circles, rectangles, and shaded parts while naming numerator and denominator, which gave students concrete sights to match the words.",
  "horizon_title": "Invite student choices",
  "horizon_note": "Offer small, concrete choices (which shape to partition, which parts to shade, or which student explains a model) so learners use their own words and methods instead of only repeating yours.",
  "journey_note": "In this short lesson you moved from simple shape ID to naming numerators and denominators and reached a solid 68% peak — you keep bringing clarity and care each session.",
  "score_framing": "A 68% today showed real strengths (visuals, vocabulary) and a clear, reachable next step toward more student choice and voice.",
  "domain_whys": {
    "high_leverage_practices": "This is developing because you asked 'جو اوپر جو نمبر ہوتا ہے، اس کو کیا کہتے ہیں؟' and students replied 'نمبریٹر', which created accurate but brief teacher-led responses — it's not full marks because students were not invited to choose a model or method to represent or explain a fraction themselves.",
    "student_engagement": "This is strong because you used the cake anchor and counting moments like 'ہم پارٹ کرتے ہیں' to keep attention and make the idea relatable — it's not full marks because students asked procedural or logistical questions (for example, 'میم، ٹیسٹ لکھیں؟') instead of asking content questions about fractions.",
    "teacher_subject_knowledge": "This is strong because you corrected placement of numerator and denominator in exchanges like 'ٹھیک ہے، 9 ہم نے اوپر لکھنا ہے، اور 10؟' — it's not full marks because you did not press students to explain their reasoning beyond one-word answers."
  }
}
